Fixes to 9front's own code, as patches against the release the build
takes (tools/release.conf: 9front-11952), to send upstream.  The patched
file is in this tree too, at the same path, made from the release's copy
with the diff:
  cp build/release/sys/src/cmd/hjfs/auth.c sys/src/cmd/hjfs/auth.c
  patch -p1 <patches/9front/hjfs-auth.diff
tools/tree applies every patch to the release and fails when this tree's
copy is not the result.

hjfs-auth.diff	hjfs/auth.c: validuser measured a name as n - p, which is
		never USERLEN or more, so any name passed and the strcpy()s
		after it overran name, lead (the stack) and memb (the heap);
		lookupuid and ingroup could read one past the end of their
		arrays when the uid was above every one (the binary search ends
		with i == n).  2026-10-02.

libc-9syscall-wasm32.diff
		libc/9syscall/mkfile: wasm32 has no system call instruction;
		its stubs are C, each a call of the platform's _trap with the
		call's number and its arguments in memory (3c's convention),
		compiled by $CC.  The other machines' assembler as it was.
		2026-10-04.

libthread-wasm32.diff
		libthread/mkfile: wasm32's machine part is its own -
		wasm32/wasm32.c, main.c, sched.c (contexts, not longjmp) - in
		place of $objtype.c, main.c and sched.c; the others as they
		were.  2026-10-04.

inst-bootsetup-9fat.diff
		rc/bin/inst/bootsetup: the machine's other boot files,
		/$cputype/9fat/*, onto the boot FAT with the kernel and
		plan9.ini (wasm32: the kernel's boot file system and the page
		that is its firmware; no other machine has the directory).
		2026-10-06.

The libc and libthread ones are for building wasm32 with 9front's mkfiles, with this
tree's libc/wasm32/mkfile and libmp's, libsec's empty wasm32/mkfiles.
wasm32 is not in 9front's CPUS: mk installall builds the CPUS, and wasm32
is built by objtype=wasm32 mk install where it is wanted.
